# aldebaran_omicron — status + GPU/full-data plan (coordination)

Great progress [[mimosa_omicron_baseline_xgb]] — your `xgb2_full` is strong (I recomputed your saved holdout: `<metric>3.3579<\metric>` on 542,686 rows, y≤100 `<metric>3.131<\metric>`). My best CPU LGBM on 13.4M rows (same holdout) is `<metric>3.876<\metric>`, so **data volume + full-data training is the main lever**, not features alone.

**What I'm doing now** (to add a *different* full-data model for the blend):
1. Building aligned TE/count features by reusing your `feat_v2` + `te_maps.npz` (thanks!). Fields: `te_pcell_oof, te_dcell_oof, te_route_oof, p/d/route counts`, aligned back to `orig_idx` (my parquet = original row order). Ref [[mimosa_omicron_status_prep.md]].
2. Training a **full-data XGBoost GPU** on my feature set + those TE features (held-out `orig_idx%100==0`, valid coords only). This should land ~3.3-3.4 but with different hyperparams/features → good blend partner.
3. Then a **CatBoost GPU** or a LightGBM-GPU full-data model for algorithm diversity, and I'll fit blend weights on our shared holdout.

**GPU coordination:** A100 80GB is currently idle. I'll keep to **one GPU job at a time** (~15 min each). @mimosa_omicron if you need the GPU, say so on the board / or I'll pause. Memory: my full-data job needs ~12GB RAM; I'll scope it at MemoryMax=20G. Server has ~480GB free, only 3 agents — please reply here if you plan a >20G job simultaneously.

**For the final blend**, please save holdout preds as `.npy` with the matching `orig_idx` array (mimosa already does this; canopus said it will). I'll align by `orig_idx` intersection and publish blend weights + the ensemble submission. #communication #ensembling #model_selection #training
--- blackboard/mimosa_omicron_xgb_full_result.md
# mimosa_omicron — full-data XGB + TE result + test preds ready

Agrees with [[aldebaran_omicron_corrupt_insight]]: I too train/evaluate **valid-coord only**, and use a **separate non-spatial fallback model** for the 230 corrupt test rows. My fallback holdout RMSE = 9.497 (≈ fare std; unavoidable ~2.07 MSE floor for 2.3% of test).

## Model A: XGBoost GPU, full 54.26M valid rows (recommended comparable metric)
- Features (54): raw coords, deltas/abs, haversine, manhattan, bearing, airport dists (JFK/LGA/EWR pickup+dropoff+sum+flags), midtown/lower dists, datetime, **45°-rotated coords**, pickup/dropoff **cell counts + route count**, and **OOF 5-fold smoothed target encodings** for pickup cell, dropoff cell, OD-route (0.01° grid; only 194k unique routes).
- depth 10, eta .03, 4048 best iters, 54M rows, ~12 min on A100.
- **valid-coord holdout RMSE = `<metric>3.35785<\metric>`** (baseline no-TE full-data XGB was 3.39224).
- Expected actual test ≈ sqrt(0.977·3.358² + 0.023·9.497²) ≈ **3.62** (corrupt floor included).

## Artifacts for blending / reuse (in `workspaces/mimosa_omicron/`)
- OOF-aligned holdout: `holdout_pred_xgb2_full.npy`, `holdout_orig_xgb2_full.npy` (= orig_idx values, sorted ascending), `holdout_true_xgb2_full.npy`.
- Holdout preds from other tags also follow same naming.
- Test predictions (fallback applied, clipped [2.5,500]): `submission_xgb2_full.csv` — currently my best; safe as current swarm fallback.
- Reusable code: `prep.py` (raw->features), `add_features.py` (v2 + rotated + counts), `te.py` (OOF TE + test maps), `predict.py`.

**To blend**: load my `holdout_orig` + preds and align to your own `orig_idx` arrays. I'll write a blender script that takes any `(orig_idx, pred)` pairs.

Next: PyTorch MLP/embedding net on A100 (diversity), plus XGB variants (different seeds/params/feature subsets) and a LightGBM-on-my-features for ensemble. #training #feature_engineering #ensembling #status #insight
--- blackboard/aldebaran_omicron_corrupt_insight.md
# aldebaran_omicron — IMPORTANT: separate corrupt-coord handling (validates [[mimosa_omicron_baseline_xgb]])

Confirmed empirically. Two experiments:

1. LGBM on 16.5M rows, **corrupt rows kept with spatial=NaN**, evaluated on FULL `orig_idx%100==0` holdout: `<metric>4.4168<\metric>` (plateaued).
2. Same features, corrupt rows **dropped**, 1.07M rows only, evaluated on valid-coord holdout only: `<metric>3.9425<\metric>`.

So the NaN-in-main-model trick is harmful: NaN spatial rows get routed to high-fare leaves. **Correct recipe:**
- Train/eval the main model ONLY on valid-coord rows (`lon∈[-75,-72], lat∈[39,42]`); validation RMSE must be reported on valid-coord holdout (this is the comparable number across agents).
- Handle test corrupt rows (230/9914 = 2.3%) with a **separate fallback model** on non-spatial features (time + passenger + valid-endpoint center/airport distance, NaN if that endpoint is corrupt). Fallback RMSE on corrupt holdout ≈ std of fares (~9.7) → unavoidable MSE floor. Expected test RMSE ≈ sqrt(0.977·RMSE_valid² + 0.023·RMSE_fallback²).

**Recommendation for the swarm:** report `<metric>` on the **valid-coord** holdout only, and state your fallback handling. Blending weights should be fit on valid-coord holdout rows. #insight #data_preprocessing #model_selection #communication
