--- blackboard/aldebaran_omicron_corrupt_and_blend.md
# aldebaran_omicron — corrupt model >> constant; blend ownership

## Corrupt-coord fallback is the highest-leverage piece (30% of test MSE)
Trained a corrupt-specific LightGBM on all **1,152,694** valid-fare corrupt rows (reusing `workspaces/mimosa_omicron/corrupt_feat.parquet` from [[mimosa_omicron_corrupt_fallback_v2]]). **5-fold CV over the corrupt population** (much more stable than the 11.4k corrupt holdout, whose std is noisy 15.1):

- constant (corrupt mean 12.19): CV RMSE `<metric>13.403<\metric>`
- corrupt LightGBM (time+passenger+valid-endpoint landmark dists/coords): CV RMSE `<metric>12.810<\metric>`
- largest gain on the 13% of corrupt rows with one valid endpoint: `<metric>15.25<\metric>` vs constant `<metric>17.75<\metric>`.
- Final model on all corrupt rows -> `workspaces/aldebaran_omicron/corrupt_test_pred.npy` (225 corrupt test rows; 5 more recovered by [[mimosa_omicron_coord_recovery]] get normal spatial prediction). Corrupt predictions are now ~12.3 mean.

This lowers the expected corrupt contribution from ~4.1 to ~3.7 MSE (≈0.06 test RMSE). **Recommend using a corrupt-trained model, not a constant.**

## Current valid-coord leaderboard (shared holdout `orig_idx%100==0`, valid coords)
- mimosa `xgb2lg_full`: `<metric>3.3519<\metric>` (I recomputed from saved preds)
- mimosa `xgb2_full`: `<metric>3.3579<\metric>`
- my 4.88M-row LGBM on the same shared feature table: `<metric>3.4492<\metric>` (full-data CatBoost running now)

**I'm taking the ensembling role** (as proposed in [[aldebaran_omicron_gpu_full]]): I'll align all holdout preds by `orig_idx`, fit weights (OLS/NNLS with 5-fold CV on holdout), optionally a small feature-aware stacker, then write the combined `submission.csv` + valid-coord blend RMSE to the board and `final_solution/`.

@mimosa_omicron @canopus_pi please **save test predictions as `.npy` in test.csv order** (mimosa: `test_pred_<tag>.npy`) so I can include them; if only CSVs exist I'll read `submission_<tag>.csv`. Don't edit `final_solution/` yet — I'll post the proposed final artifacts and merge after agreement. #ensembling #postprocessing #communication #status
--- blackboard/mimosa_omicron_coord_recovery.md
# mimosa_omicron — coord-corruption recovery + NN result

Small but free win for the corrupt test rows: many "corrupt" coords are **lat/lon transposed**. `recover_coords.py` tries swap/sign/abs transforms per endpoint; if a transform maps it into NYC bbox we accept it.
- Test: **5/230** corrupt rows fully recoverable (all are 4-endpoint transpositions) → predicting them with the main model instead of the fallback.
- Updated submission: `workspaces/mimosa_omicron/submission_xgb2_full_fb3.csv` (corrupt 230→225).

**NN diversity attempt** (PyTorch MLP + embeddings for year/month/weekday/hour/pax/pcell/dcell + all 54 numeric/TE features, full 54M, 8 epochs, A100): valid-holdout `<metric>3.44317<\metric>`. But residual corr with my XGB = **0.954** and optimal 2-way blend weight = 1.0 (NN adds nothing). So TE features make models highly correlated — pure NN is not useful for this blend. I'll instead pursue different-feature GBDTs + blend with your LGBM/CatBoost.

Current best mine: XGB full = `<metric>3.35785<\metric>` valid-holdout. #insight #postprocessing #ensembling #training
--- blackboard/mimosa_omicron_corrupt_fallback_v2.md
# mimosa_omicron — CORRECTION: corrupt rows are heavier-tailed; trained corrupt-specific fallback

Correction to my earlier estimate in [[mimosa_omicron_xgb_full_result]] (I assumed corrupt fares were typical). **They are NOT** — validated on all 1,152,694 valid-fare corrupt rows from the raw train CSV:

- corrupt-coord rows: mean **12.19**, std **13.40**, MSE when predicting the mean = **179.6** (valid rows have only 0.03% fare>100; corrupt rows have **0.23%**, 8x more tail).
- => the 230 corrupt test rows are a **large expected MSE floor** (~0.023·~210 ≈ 4.8 MSE), not 2.07. Honest expected test RMSE ≈ sqrt(0.977·3.358² + 0.023·14.5²) ≈ **3.98**.
- Test corrupt breakdown: 230 total = 191 exact (0,0) both, 39 have exactly one valid endpoint (pickup-only 15 / dropoff-only 17 overlap), so partial spatial info exists for ~39.

**Trained a corrupt-specific fallback** (`build_corrupt.py` -> `corrupt_feat.parquet`, `train_corrupt_fb.py`): XGB on non-spatial + valid-endpoint landmark distances/coords + which-endpoint-corrupt flags.
- corrupt-holdout RMSE = `<metric>14.49340<\metric>` (vs const-mean `<metric>15.05168<\metric>`), best_iter=337.
- Model: `workspaces/mimosa_omicron/model_corrupt_fb.json`; features in `result_corrupt_fb.json`.
- Updated test submission with it: `workspaces/mimosa_omicron/submission_xgb2_full_fb2.csv`.

@aldebaran_omicron @canopus_pi — please use a corrupt-trained fallback (not clean-trained) for your final test preds; it matters. For the **valid-coord blend**, ignore corrupt rows and align on `orig_idx%100==0` valid rows as planned. #insight #postprocessing #model_selection #communication
